  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows an upper-level low over \nnorthern Utah slowly translating south and east, with associated\ncloud cover gradually eroding from north to south across \neastern Idaho. A few light sprinkles and passing showers beneath\nthe thicker cloud decks produced only trace amounts to a few \nhundredths of an inch of precipitation at best. Clearing \nconditions will continue through late afternoon and early \nevening, though this window will be relatively short-lived.\n\nA weak shortwave trough swinging across the panhandle will \ndirect another round of mid-to-high level clouds over the \nforecast area toward daybreak Thursday. High-resolution REFS \nguidance depicts isolated light precipitation developing \nThursday afternoon across the Central Mountains and into the \nSoutheast Highlands in proximity to the track of the wave. \nHowever, low-level moisture remains limited, and the vast \nmajority of the region will remain dry.\n\nAs the passing shortwave departs to the east Thursday night, \noperational models and ensemble clusters remain in strong \nconsensus that an upper-level ridge will amplify over the \nnorthern Great Basin and northern Rockies. This ridge will \nremain firmly anchored over the Intermountain West to close out \nthe workweek, throughout the upcoming weekend, and into the \nmiddle of next week. Strong subsidence will keep skies mostly \nclear to partly cloudy and maintain completely dry conditions \nacross eastern Idaho, with no organized or impactful weather \nsystems in sight through the 7-day period.\n\nTemperatures over the next couple of days will hover near\nseasonal climatology. While overnight low temperatures have been\nrunning slightly above normal due to periodic cloud insulation,\ndaytime maximums will stay within a few degrees of early\nNovember averages through Friday. A notable warming trend takes\nhold over the weekend beneath rising 500 mb heights. Daytime\nhighs will climb 7 to 10 degrees above seasonal norms by Sunday\nand persist into early next week. This will push afternoon\ntemperatures into the upper 50s to near 60\u00b0F across lower-\nelevation valleys of the Snake Plain and Magic Valley, providing\npleasant, unseasonably mild fall weather.",
